ATTACK 1 -- crash-window semantics
  (a) same-filesystem rename: os.CreateTemp(baseDir, ...) stages directly in
      baseDir; configPath = filepath.Join(baseDir, configFile) is the same
      directory. Confirmed by reading the code -- no cross-fs rename risk.
      NOT REFUTED.
  (b) fsync gap: neither the old code (os.WriteFile(tmpPath,...) then
      os.Rename, `git show HEAD:internal/services/storage/auth.go`) nor the
      new code calls fsync before rename. A power-loss between rename
      landing and data reaching platter could still produce a truncated/
      empty config on some filesystems/mount options. VERIFIED PRE-EXISTING
      (both old and new code share this gap byte-for-byte) -- RECORDED AS
      OBSERVATION, NOT A REGRESSION. The window is not widened by this change.
  (c) chmod-after-close-before-rename window: `go doc os.CreateTemp` states
      the file "is created with mode 0o600 (before umask)" -- confirmed via
      stdlib doc. Umask can only clear bits, and 0600 has no group/other bits
      to clear, so there is no window, before or after the explicit chmod,
      where the staging file is more permissive than 0600. The explicit
      os.Chmod(tmpPath, 0600) is genuinely redundant, as the comment claims.
      NOT REFUTED. EncryptionConfig's fields (Method, RecipientID,
      SSHKeyPath, AgeIdentityPath, YubiKeyIdentity, YubiKeyRecipient) are
      paths/method/public-recipient identifiers, not raw salts or wrapped
      keys -- so the secrecy stakes of this file are "reveals key-file
      locations and auth method," moderate but real; 0600 is appropriate
      and, per (c), never actually breached.
  Bonus (not asked, volunteered): the OLD code's fixed, predictable staging
  name (configPath+".tmp") combined with os.WriteFile's lack of O_EXCL is a
  textbook predictable-tmpfile-name pattern; the NEW code's CreateTemp uses
  O_CREATE|O_EXCL with an unpredictable name, closing that class of issue
  incidentally. Not claimed by the report, not required, but confirms the
  fix is not merely cosmetic.

ATTACK 2 -- concurrency claim wording
  $ grep -rn 'saveConfig(' internal/services/storage/*.go | grep -v _test
    -> only definition (auth.go:99) and one call site (migration.go:93),
       inside EnableEncryptionWithProvider, which takes s.mu.Lock() at
       migration.go:25 and holds it for the whole function (no unlock/
       relock in between). So within one *Storage instance, saveConfig
       cannot race with itself today -- the "concurrent saves collide"
       framing describes the regime-level convention/consistency argument,
       not a demonstrated live bug in this codebase's actual call graph.
  The report's own wording ("What it actually buys: no fixed-name collision
  between concurrent saves...") stays at the level of what the code permits
  structurally, and does not claim to have found or fixed an exploitable
  concurrent-caller bug in production. No overclaim found. NOT REFUTED
  (recorded as a scope observation, matching the adversarial brief's
  instruction not to penalize accurately-hedged claims).

